/* Parley stylesheet - clean, minimal, fast */
/* Color palette: Warm grays with soft accent colors for comfortable long sessions */

Screen {
    background: #1e1e2e;  /* Deep blue-gray, easy on eyes */
}

/* Every dialog sits in the middle of the terminal, not in its top-left corner.
   Textual gives a ModalScreen no alignment of its own, so a dialog narrower than
   the screen anchors at 0,0 and reads as a rendering accident rather than an
   overlay. One rule rather than one per screen: the type selector matches every
   ModalScreen subclass, so a new dialog is centred the day it is written. */
ModalScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

/* === Sidebar === */
/* A share of the width, not a fixed 30 columns. 30 is 37.5% of an 80-column
   terminal — more than a third of the screen spent on a list that is often
   nothing but "No sessions yet". A quarter instead, floored at 24 (below that an
   elided session name is shorter than the words in it) and capped at 32 (one row
   per session; columns past a full title show nothing more). At 120 columns that
   is the same 30 it always was, so the wide-terminal look is unchanged. */
#sidebar {
    width: 25%;
    min-width: 24;
    max-width: 32;
    background: #181825;  /* Slightly darker than main */
    border-right: solid #45475a;  /* Subtle gray border */
}

/* One line, not three: the title says which program this is and nothing else,
   so it does not need two blank rows of its own. */
.sidebar-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cdd6f4;  /* Soft lavender for titles */
    padding: 0 1;
    background: #11111b;
}

#new-chat-button {
    margin: 1;
    width: 100%;
    background: #313244;
    color: #cdd6f4;
    border: solid #45475a;
}

#new-chat-button:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

.chat-group-header {
    margin-top: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    color: #6c7086;  /* Muted for group headers */
    text-style: italic;
}

/* One row per session, cut visibly, with a rule under it.
   A session name is a sentence and the column is ~20 columns wide, so the
   default wrap spent two or three rows on a single entry and — with no boundary
   between entries — the list stopped reading as a list at all. `text-wrap:
   nowrap` holds each name to its own row and `text-overflow: ellipsis` marks
   where it was cut (same contract as the tree browser's `_elide`: a reader can
   see that there is more). The bottom rule is the entry boundary, in the same
   quiet gray the panel host divides itself from the chat with. */
.chat-list-item {
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 0;
    color: #bac2de;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    border-bottom: solid #313244;
}

.chat-list-item:hover {
    background: #313244;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

.chat-list-empty {
    padding: 1;
    color: #6c7086;
    text-style: italic;
}

/* Vertical padding only. The horizontal column is the scarce one — 24 columns of
   sidebar already spend 1 on the border, 2 on the scroll gutter, 2 on the item's
   own padding and 2 on its bullet — and an inset that only shortens the session
   names buys nothing the item padding does not already give. */
#chat-list {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0;
}

#chat-list-content {
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* === Chat Display === */
#chat-display {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: #1e1e2e;
}

/* The empty-state pane (handoff §4.4). One color, because the label/value
   hierarchy inside it is drawn with Rich's `dim` — a RELATIVE style that
   composes with whatever color lands here, so a future FFwF theme restyles this
   pane by changing this one line and nothing in app.py. `height: auto` keeps it
   out of the way: it is a child of the scrolling chat column, not an overlay,
   and ChatDisplay hides it the moment the first message box mounts. */
#chat-placeholder {
    height: auto;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

/* === Message Styling === */
/* ONE universal widget (MessageBox) renders every message. The role selects a
   border color + label on the BOX ITSELF, so an assistant's reasoning + answer
   + tool boxes all sit inside one titled border (one completion = one box). */
.chat-message {
    margin: 1 0;
    height: auto;
    /* No VERTICAL padding: the border already separates the box from its
       neighbours, and a padding row above and below every message cost two rows
       per message for nothing. One column left and right stays, so text does not
       sit against the border glyph. */
    padding: 0 1;
    border: solid #45475a;  /* default; per-role rules below recolor */
}

/* The lazy reasoning / tools slots collapse to nothing when empty, so a plain
   user/system message looks exactly like a single text box. */
.message-reasoning, .message-tools {
    height: auto;
}

/* Textual's Markdown default is `padding: 0 2`, which sat inside the box's own
   padding and border — six columns of chrome per side before a character of
   text. The box supplies the inset now. */
.message-content {
    color: #cdd6f4;
    padding: 0;
}

/* === Markdown block spacing ===
   Textual's markdown block widgets each carry vertical spacing of their own:
   MarkdownHeader `margin: 2 0 1 0` (H3-H6 `margin: 1 0`), MarkdownParagraph
   `margin: 0 0 1 0`, MarkdownFence `margin: 1 0` plus `padding: 1 2` on its inner
   Label, MarkdownBlockQuote `margin: 1 0`, the bullet/ordered lists `margin:
   0 0 1 0`, MarkdownTable `margin-bottom: 1`, MarkdownHorizontalRule
   `padding-top: 1; margin-bottom: 1`. After the message box's own padding was
   zeroed those are the ONLY remaining blank rows in a message — a blank row above
   and below every code block, one after every paragraph, three around a heading.
   On a 24-row terminal a four-block answer spent a quarter of the screen on them.

   Every vertical value goes to zero; every horizontal value is left exactly as it
   was (the blockquote's `padding: 0 1`, the fence Label's 2 columns, the list
   items' `margin-right: 1`), because the horizontal inset is what keeps a fence
   or a quote legible as a block and costs no rows.

   CONSEQUENCE, deliberately accepted: paragraph breaks in an assistant answer are
   now carried by nothing but the sentence ending short of the right margin — two
   consecutive paragraphs abut. Headings, fences, lists and quotes still separate
   themselves visually (they are styled), so the loss is confined to prose
   paragraph against prose paragraph.

   Scoped under `.chat-message` — the MessageBox — so it covers all four Markdown
   widgets τ mounts (the message body, a reasoning region, a tool box's args and
   result, all of which live inside a MessageBox) and reaches no Markdown widget
   outside the transcript. */
.chat-message MarkdownH1,
.chat-message MarkdownH2,
.chat-message MarkdownH3,
.chat-message MarkdownH4,
.chat-message MarkdownH5,
.chat-message MarkdownH6,
.chat-message MarkdownParagraph,
.chat-message MarkdownBlockQuote,
.chat-message MarkdownBulletList,
.chat-message MarkdownOrderedList,
.chat-message MarkdownList,
.chat-message MarkdownListItem,
.chat-message MarkdownTable,
.chat-message MarkdownFence {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
}

/* The fence's inner Label is where its `padding: 1 2` lives — a blank row above
   and below the code itself, on top of the fence's own margin. Two columns of
   left/right inset stay. */
.chat-message MarkdownFence > Label {
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* A blockquote's inner BlockQuote carries `margin-top: 1` (its `margin-left: 2`
   is the horizontal inset that makes it read as a quote — kept). */
.chat-message MarkdownBlockQuote > BlockQuote {
    margin-top: 0;
}

/* A rule is a one-row border-bottom with a row of padding above it and a row of
   margin below; the line itself is the separator. */
.chat-message MarkdownHorizontalRule {
    padding-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* Pending placeholder — muted, until it resolves into a real role */
.box-pending {
    border: solid #6c7086;  /* Muted gray */
    color: #6c7086;
}

.box-user { border: solid #89dceb; }       /* calm cyan */
.box-assistant { border: solid #f9e2af; }  /* warm amber */
.box-system { border: solid #cba6f7; }     /* subtle purple */
.box-toolCall { border: solid #fab387; }   /* orange (legacy standalone tool box) */
.box-toolResult { border: solid #a6e3a1; margin: 1 0 1 2; }  /* green, indented */
.box-error { border: solid #f38ba8; }      /* red */
.box-blocked { border: solid #f9e2af; }    /* amber — an extension veto (S50), not a tool failure */

/* === Foreign lanes (B3-b) === */
/* Every box belonging to a turn this frontend did not originate — a bus/timer
   submission's bubble, a forked sub-agent's steps, the answer promoted out of
   either. ONE rule for all of them (including a source this build has never
   heard of), so "not the main line" is a single glance; the border TITLE says
   which source, and the border SUBTITLE who. Dashed + mauve so it reads as
   adjacent to the conversation rather than part of it. */
.chat-message.lane-foreign {
    border: dashed #b4a7f5;
    margin: 1 0 1 2;
}

.chat-message.lane-foreign > .message-content {
    color: #bac2de;
}

/* === Collapsible density (reasoning regions, tool boxes, exchanges) ===
   Textual's Collapsible spends three rows on a COLLAPSED one: a `border-top:
   hkey` rule, the title, and a `padding-bottom: 1`. Stacked — an exchange
   holding a step holding two tool calls — that is most of a screen of chrome
   around a handful of title lines. Every collapsed collapsible is one row here,
   and an expanded one puts its content flush against the box. */
.reasoning-region, .tool-box, .exchange-box {
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
}

/* ONE column of left indent inside an expanded collapsible, and nothing on the
   other three sides. Nesting is otherwise conveyed only by the enclosing border,
   so a `▼ Thought` nested inside an answer and a `▼ ✓ read(…)` that is a SIBLING
   of that answer sat at the same left offset and read as siblings. The signal a
   reader actually uses is the vertical alignment of the `▼` markers; one column
   is enough to break it, and on an 80-column terminal it costs 1.25% of the line
   — cheap next to the two rows Textual's own Collapsible padding used to cost. */
.reasoning-region > Contents,
.tool-box > Contents,
.exchange-box > Contents {
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

/* === Reasoning / thinking regions (collapsible, dim+italic like pi) === */
.reasoning-region {
    margin: 0;
}

.reasoning-region > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #9399b2;  /* muted gray */
    text-style: italic;
}

.reasoning-region Markdown {
    color: #9399b2;
    text-style: italic;
}

/* === Tool call + result boxes (one collapsible per call) === */
.tool-box {
    margin: 0;
}

.tool-box > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #fab387;  /* soft orange, matches the old tool-call color */
}

.tool-box.box-error > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #f38ba8;  /* soft red on error */
}

.tool-box.box-blocked > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #f9e2af;  /* amber — a `tool_call` extension veto (S50), read as a policy block */
}

/* === Exchange grouping (one user→answer span; summary line title) === */
.exchange-box {
    margin: 1 0;
}

.exchange-box > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #cdd6f4;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* A foreign lane's exchange (B3-a set the class; B3-b gives it the rule it never
   had). Same mauve as the boxes inside it, so the whole span reads as one
   not-the-main-line unit. */
.exchange-box.exchange-foreign > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #b4a7f5;
}

/* === Input Styling === */
#chat-input {
    margin: 0 1 1 1;
    border: solid #45475a;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 10;
    background: #181825;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#chat-input:focus {
    border: solid #89dceb;  /* Matches user message color */
    background: #1e1e2e;
}

/* === System Prompt Editor Modal === */
/* 80x25 flat, which is one row taller than an 80x24 terminal and one column wider
   than its usable width: the buttons rendered off the bottom edge, where they
   cannot be clicked. The preferred size is kept — an editor wants room — but it is
   now a ceiling rather than a demand, and the `1fr` textarea absorbs whatever the
   clamp takes away. */
#prompt-editor-dialog {
    width: 80;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 25;
    max-height: 100%;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 1;
}

#prompt-editor-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#prompt-editor-textarea {
    height: 1fr;
    margin-bottom: 1;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#prompt-editor-textarea:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

#prompt-editor-buttons {
    height: auto;
    align: center middle;
}

#prompt-editor-buttons Button {
    margin: 0 1;
    background: #313244;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#prompt-editor-buttons Button:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

/* Consequence line above the RollbackPromptModal's editor — the operation
   discards visible work, so it says so before the prompt is typed. */
#rollback-help {
    height: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1;
    color: #a6adc8;
}

/* === Session Tree Browser Modal === */
/* Fills the screen. It was 90x30 regardless of terminal size, which left a dead
   band on a wide terminal and rendered off-screen (clipped top and bottom) on an
   80x24 one. The tree is the widest thing in the app — every row is a preview
   sentence — so it gets the whole width there is. */
#tree-browser-dialog {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 0 1;
}

#tree-browser-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

/* Rows on top, the highlighted node in full below — STACKED, not side by side.
   A column split gave both halves about half the terminal's width, and both of
   them are text that has to wrap: the tree rows elided down to a few characters
   and the pane's prose broke every five or six words. Stacking spends rows
   instead, which is the cheaper currency here — a tree row is one line whatever
   its width, so the tree loses only the count of nodes it can show at once,
   while the pane gets the full width its markdown wants. `1fr`/`1fr`: even
   halves. Below `SessionTreeModal.DETAIL_MIN_HEIGHT` there are not enough rows
   to halve, and the pane hides. */
#tree-browser-body {
    height: 1fr;
}

#tree-browser-tree {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1fr;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#tree-browser-tree:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

#tree-detail {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1fr;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    padding: 0 1;
}

#tree-detail:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

/* "⋯ 3 earlier" / "⋯ 2 branches from here, 5 later" — the rows that keep the
   pane from reading as a three-message conversation. Muted and centered so they
   read as a fold marker rather than as another message. */
#tree-detail .detail-fold {
    color: #6c7086;
    text-align: center;
    text-style: italic;
}

/* === Detail-pane context messages === */
/* The neighbours of the selected node, which must recede without becoming
   unidentifiable — the reader still tells a user turn from an assistant one at a
   glance, so the role hue is KEPT and only its strength drops.

   `opacity`, not a second table of hand-picked dim hex values, and not
   `color`/`text-opacity`. A box's body is not all plain text: a tool call's
   arguments and a tool result are syntax-highlighted by Rich, which writes
   per-token colors no CSS `color` can reach, and neither `color` nor
   `text-opacity` reaches a CHILD widget (the Markdown blocks, the tool boxes)
   because neither is an inherited property. `opacity` composites the whole
   subtree against the background, so border, title, prose and highlighted code
   all fade by the same amount and every `.box-*` rule above keeps working
   unchanged. */
.chat-message.detail-context {
    opacity: 55%;
}

#tree-browser-help {
    text-align: center;
    color: #6c7086;
    margin-top: 1;
}

/* === Tree three-mode chooser Modal === */
/* `height: auto` alone is not a fit: five 3-row buttons plus their margins, the
   title and the chrome come to 26 rows, two more than an 80x24 terminal has, and
   the last two modes rendered off the bottom. Auto still picks the height on a
   terminal that can hold the list — dropping the inter-button margin below brings
   that to 21, so 80x24 is now a terminal that can. `max-height` caps it on one
   that cannot (a sixth mode, or a 15-row window), and `overflow-y: auto`
   (Container defaults to `hidden`, i.e. clip and say nothing) turns what no longer
   fits into something the reader can scroll to rather than lose. */
#tree-mode-dialog {
    width: 60;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 100%;
    overflow-y: auto;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 1;
}

#tree-mode-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#tree-mode-buttons {
    height: auto;
    align: center middle;
}

/* No margin between the buttons: each already carries its own border, so the
   blank row between them bought separation that was already there and cost five
   rows — the five that pushed the dialog past an 80x24 screen. */
#tree-mode-buttons Button {
    width: 100%;
    background: #313244;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#tree-mode-buttons Button:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

/* === Extension dialogs (confirm / select / input) — E7 §3 / S47 === */
/* Same ceiling the two tree dialogs learned to keep: a fixed 70 columns is wider
   than a 60-column terminal and the select list's fixed 30 rows are taller than an
   80x24 one, and an off-screen dialog is not a dialog. These are not scenes, so
   test_tui_appearance's modal guard cannot see them — the bound is the guard. */
#ext-confirm-dialog, #ext-select-dialog, #ext-input-dialog {
    width: 70;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 100%;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 1;
}

#ext-select-dialog {
    height: 30;
}

#ext-confirm-title, #ext-select-title, #ext-input-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#ext-confirm-message {
    margin-bottom: 1;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#ext-confirm-buttons, #ext-input-buttons {
    height: auto;
    align: center middle;
}

#ext-confirm-buttons Button, #ext-input-buttons Button {
    margin: 0 1;
    background: #313244;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#ext-confirm-buttons Button:hover, #ext-input-buttons Button:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

#ext-select-list {
    height: 1fr;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#ext-select-list:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

#ext-select-help {
    text-align: center;
    color: #6c7086;
    margin-top: 1;
}

#ext-input-field {
    margin-bottom: 1;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#ext-input-field:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

/* === Extension shortcut chord (which-key menu) — E10 §6 / S69 === */
#ext-chord-dialog {
    width: 70;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 90%;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 1;
}

#ext-chord-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

.ext-chord-entry {
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

/* === Extension form (declarative fields) — E10 §6 / S66 === */
#ext-form-dialog {
    width: 70;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 90%;
    background: #181825;
    border: thick #45475a;
    padding: 1;
}

#ext-form-title {
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#ext-form-fields {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 24;
}

.ext-form-label {
    color: #a6adc8;
    margin-top: 1;
}

.ext-form-input {
    background: #1e1e2e;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

.ext-form-input:focus {
    border: solid #cba6f7;
}

#ext-form-help {
    text-align: center;
    color: #6c7086;
    margin-top: 1;
}

#ext-form-buttons {
    height: auto;
    align: center middle;
    margin-top: 1;
}

#ext-form-buttons Button {
    margin: 0 1;
    background: #313244;
    border: solid #45475a;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

#ext-form-buttons Button:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

/* === Foreign-lane strip (B3-b) === */
/* One line naming every lane in flight that the user did not type, just above
   the extension status strip. Hidden (display:none, set in code) whenever the
   only thing running is this frontend's own turn — or nothing is. */
#lane-strip {
    height: 1;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: #181825;
    color: #b4a7f5;
}

/* === Extension status strip (E10 §6 / S67) === */
/* One-line keyed status slots from ctx.ui.set_status(), just above the Footer.
   Hidden (display:none, set in code) until an extension writes a slot. */
#ext-status-bar {
    height: 1;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: #181825;
    color: #a6adc8;
    text-style: dim;
}

/* === Extension panel host (E10 §6 / S68) === */
/* Persistent keyed panels from ctx.ui.panel(), docked to the right of the main
   area. Hidden (display:none, set in code) until an extension opens a panel.

   A share of the width, not a fixed 40 columns. At 80 columns a fixed 40 left
   the chat 8 columns, 4 of them ChatDisplay padding — the primary content
   reduced to four usable columns by a secondary surface. 30% instead, floored at
   26 (a panel's table wants four short columns inside a round border and a
   column of host padding per side) and capped at 44 (past that a keyed status
   panel is mostly whitespace, and the chat should have the rest).

   Together with #sidebar this leaves the chat 45% of the width in the middle of
   the range, less 2 columns of scrollbar gutter and 4 of ChatDisplay padding.
   Where that stops clearing the 40-column floor asserted in
   tests/test_tui_appearance.py is Parley.SIDE_COLUMNS_MIN_WIDTH, below which the
   sidebar yields rather than the chat. */
#ext-panel-host {
    width: 30%;
    min-width: 26;
    max-width: 44;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: #181825;
    border-left: solid #313244;
}

.ext-panel {
    height: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    border: round #45475a;
}

.ext-panel-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: #cba6f7;
}

.ext-panel-body {
    color: #cdd6f4;
    margin-top: 1;
}

.ext-panel-actions {
    height: auto;
    margin-top: 1;
}

/* An equal share of the row each, rather than each sizing to its own label.
   A Button defaults to `width: auto`, which here is 16 columns; two of them plus
   the margin want 33, and since the panel became a share of the terminal
   (`#ext-panel-host: 30%`, floor 26) its content row is about 30 columns at
   120x40 and 19 at 80x24. The Horizontal does not wrap — it clips — so the
   second button was cut in half on a wide terminal and off the screen entirely
   on a narrow one, silently, because a clipped widget still reports its full
   size. `1fr` makes the row divide by however many actions a panel declares,
   which is a number this stylesheet does not get to know.

   `min-width: 0` is the load-bearing half. Textual's Button carries
   `width: auto; min-width: 16`, and the floor outranks the share — with `1fr`
   alone each button still measured 17 columns in a 19-column row, i.e. exactly
   the bug, because a min-width a container cannot honour is not reported as an
   error, it is just drawn past the edge. */
.ext-panel-action {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 0;
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    margin: 0 1 0 0;
    background: #313244;
    border: none;
    color: #cdd6f4;
}

.ext-panel-action:hover {
    background: #45475a;
}

/* === Code blocks in markdown === */
Markdown CodeBlock {
    background: #11111b;
    border: solid #313244;
    color: #a6e3a1;  /* Soft green for code */
}

/* Tool-call / tool-result blocks are rendered by the shared MessageBox widget
   (see the `.box-toolCall` / `.box-toolResult` rules above), not by a separate
   Static widget. */
